export const body = "<p>If you have looked at graphical settings in games, you will notice one setting that appears in many games: VSync. Some of them come with a tool-tip stating: “Turn on VSync if you suffer from screen tearing.\"</p>\n\n<p>Screen tearing is a visual artifact that produces a “torn” look on your screen. In the opposite picture of <em>Dragon Age: Inquisition</em>, the character is cut in half and the edges of cliffs fail to line up. You can see a boundary, which is also called a \"tear line,\" between what seems to be two images. This issue gets even worse in the <em>Mass Effect Legendary Edition</em> screenshot, which appears to be spliced from four images.</p>\n\n<p>Screen tearing happens when the monitor displays information from multiple frames/images when rendering a single frame.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a2c4a4d284b086fd449d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a2c4a4d284b086fd449d.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"347\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/8b4b8c0e5e2d60461bea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/8b4b8c0e5e2d60461bea.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"350\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/88ca2212810520195f74.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/88ca2212810520195f74.webp\" width=\"700\" height=\"438\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/29170e41c807c1c0d15f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/29170e41c807c1c0d15f.webp\" width=\"1094\" height=\"695\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Ideally, the monitor displays frames one by one, and there's a cap for the number of frames per second. That cap is called \"refresh rate.\" So, when the monitor receives more frames than the cap, it has to render multiple at the same time, resulting in screen tearing.</p>\n\n<p>\"Refresh rate” originates from good old CRT monitors. The electron beams CRTs use to light their phosphor-coated screens must move to scan the entire screen, as illustrated in the top picture. These electron beams go from top to bottom and then return to the top-left corner during the so-called vertical blanking interval (VBI). Monitors don't receive images during this interval. As new data is sent to monitors after the VBI, the screen is re-scanned to display a new image.</p>\n\n<p>Although modern monitors work differently, we still use the term \"refresh rate\" today. Although no electron beam movement is involved, there is still a VBI, during which time monitors will not display incoming data.</p>\n\n<p>The most common solution to screen tearing is multiple buffering. Imagine you want to use a bucket to fill a paddling pool with water. With one bucket, you have to fill the bucket, turn your tap off, walk over to the pool, pour the water in, and then go back to the tap. Using an extra bucket allows you to put it under the tap while you walk over to the pool with the first. You only have to switch the buckets when you return. This is what double buffering is. In the opposite picture, the rectangle on the left represents the tap and the one on the right is the paddling pool. Square A and square B represent the buckets.</p>\n\n<p>Triple buffering adds one more bucket and another person. Try to figure out how that works.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ac827ceb9a7d81ae3a5d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ac827ceb9a7d81ae3a5d.webp\" width=\"819\" height=\"666\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/4aa1c409acf8ee46a62e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/4aa1c409acf8ee46a62e.webp\" width=\"494\" height=\"371\" style=\"--source-width:494px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Vertical Synchronization (VSync) is one such buffering technique. There are two buffers, one of them being the off-screen buffer and the other being the active buffer. After drawing to the off-screen buffer, your GPU either copies the off-screen buffer into the active buffer (double buffering) or switches between two buffers (page flipping) so that the off-screen buffer turns active. This is where VSync kicks in: it ensures that this copying/swapping process only happens during the VBI. Now that GPU never renders to the active buffer and updates the screen while it is being displayed, the monitor will always show full frames. As a result, VSync locks your game FPS (frames per second) at the monitor’s refresh rate.</p>\n\n<p>However, that becomes a major problem when your game renders at a frame rate below the refresh rate. Suppose your game runs at 45FPS on a 60Hz monitor. Because the monitor needs to wait for the GPU to update the next frame, it has to display the same frame again.</p>\n\n<p>When the GPU finishes rendering, VSync prevents it from updating the active buffer. So, the monitor displays the first frame again, and the next frame has to wait for the next refresh cycle. Because only one frame gets displayed in two refresh cycles, your game is further slowed down to 30FPS, half of 60. This is also known as screen stuttering. Triple buffering is thus introduced to address that setback at a higher GPU cost.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/20d072daf6b97245fb0a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/20d072daf6b97245fb0a.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"341\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/046af39b9702755e522b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/046af39b9702755e522b.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>You can adjust the refresh rate of your monitor to make it synchronize with the frame rate. Monitors with variable refresh rates can adapt to the GPU's frame rate. Nvidia’s GPUs also allow you to use Adaptive Sync. This feature enables VSync at higher frame rates to eliminate tearing and disables VSync at lower frame rates to minimize stuttering.</p>\n\n<p>To sum up, VSync is a technology that deals with the mismatch between refresh rates and frame rates. While it's effective against screen tearing, it also causes stuttering when your frame rate is lower than refresh rate. Thus, you should switch VSync on or off based on your hardware. Or, you can utilize newer technologies such as Adaptive Sync or variable refresh rate monitors.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy gaming!</p>";
